e class=3D"style_learnContent__K5K7M"><header><h1>Product Array Without Cur=
rent Element</h1></header><div class=3D"style_problemDescription__T4cPe"><p=
>Given an array of integers, return an array res so that <code>res[i]</code=
> is equal to the product of all the elements of the input array except <co=
de>nums[i]</code> itself.</p><h4 id=3D"example">Example:</h4><pre><code cla=
ss=3D"hljs language-python">Input: nums =3D [<span class=3D"hljs-number">2<=
/span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">1=
</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">4</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">=
5</span>]
Output: [<span class=3D"hljs-number">60</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number"=
>40</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">120</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-num=
ber">30</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">24</span>]
</code></pre><p>Explanation: The output value at index 0 is the product of =
all numbers except <code>nums[0]</code> (3=E2=8B=851=E2=8B=854=E2=8B=855 =
=3D 60). The same logic applies to the rest of the output.</p></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>The straightforward solution to this problem is to find the total produc=
t of the array and divide it by each of the values in nums individually to =
get the output array:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a calculation demonstrating a coding patter=
n.  The left side shows an array `[2, 3, 1, 4, 5]` with each element visual=
ly connected via orange curved lines to a central node labeled 'product =3D=
 120'. This indicates that the product of all elements in the array is 120.=
 An arrow points to the right, leading to a calculation where 120 is divide=
d by each element of the original array: `res =3D [120/2, 120/3, 120/1, 120=
/4, 120/5]`.  Below this, the results of these divisions are shown as a new=
 array: `res =3D [60, 40, 120, 30, 24]`.  The diagram visually illustrates =
the process of calculating the result of dividing a product by each of its =
factors." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"551" height=3D"92" decoding=3D"async" d=
ata-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns=
/prefix-sums/product-array-without-current-element/image-10-03-1-N4GBWUNI.s=
vg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div></figure>
<p>This approach allows us to solve the problem in linear time and constant=
 space. However, a potential follow-up question by an interviewer is: <stro=
ng>what if we can=E2=80=99t use division?</strong> Let=E2=80=99s explore a =
solution to this.</p>
<p><strong>Avoiding division</strong><br>
A brute force approach involves calculating the output value for each index=
 one by one. This would take <span class=3D"katex"><span class=3D"katex-mat=
hml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><m=
i>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo=
></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-tex">O(n)</annotation></seman=
tics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span cl=
ass=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height: 1em; vertical-align: -0=
.25em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.027=
78em;">O</span><span class=3D"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal=
">n</span><span class=3D"mclose">)</span></span></span></span> time per ind=
ex, leading to an overall time complexity of <span class=3D"katex"><span cl=
ass=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><se=
mantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=3D"false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2<=
/mn></msup><mo stretchy=3D"false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"appl=
ication/x-tex">O(n^2)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"=
katex-html" aria-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut"=
 style=3D"height: 1.0641em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class=3D=
"mord mathnormal" style=3D"margin-right: 0.02778em;">O</span><span class=3D=
"mopen">(</span><span class=3D"mord"><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</spa=
n><span class=3D"msupsub"><span class=3D"vlist-t"><span class=3D"vlist-r"><=
span class=3D"vlist" style=3D"height: 0.8141em;"><span style=3D"top: -3.063=
em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class=3D"pstrut" style=3D"height: 2.7em;">=
</span><span class=3D"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class=3D"mord =
mtight">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span clas=
s=3D"mclose">)</span></span></span></span>, where <span class=3D"katex"><sp=
an class=3D"katex-mathml"><math xmlns=3D"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML=
"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=3D"application/x-t=
ex">n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=3D"katex-html" aria=
-hidden=3D"true"><span class=3D"base"><span class=3D"strut" style=3D"height=
: 0.4306em;"></span><span class=3D"mord mathnormal">n</span></span></span><=
/span> denotes the length of the array. This is inefficient, so let's look =
at other approaches.</p>
<p>An important insight is that the output for any given index can be deter=
mined by multiplying two things:</p>
<ol>
<li>The product of all numbers to the left of the index.</li>
<li>The product of all numbers to the right of the index.</li>
</ol>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a diagram illustrating a coding pattern, li=
kely related to array processing or a similar data structure.  A top-level =
